  "transcript": "There is a band under your kneecap about as wide as your thumb and for its size it is one of the strongest things in your body. It is so good at its job that nobody ever thinks to check it, not even the person who read your scan. That band is the patella tendon. It sits two centimetres below your kneecap on the front of the joint and every step you take lands on it. Put your finger there now and press. That is the one. It is not a big thing. It is about as wide as your thumb and it has been quietly taking your whole body weight multiplied since you were a teenager. Going up the stairs, your muscles lift you. Going down, nothing lifts you. You are catching yourself on every step so coming down puts more through that band than going up does. And inside the joint there is a layer of cartilage doing the absorbing. And over the years that layer thins. That part is ordinary. It happens to everybody. But here is what nobody explains. The load does not thin with it. 17 times your body weight is still arriving every step in exactly the same place. The cushion gets thinner. The weight stays exactly the same. That is why it feels like it arrived overnight. Nothing about the way you walk changed so you assume nothing changed. And here is the part that catches people out. You do not have to have done anything to your knees for this to happen. Some of the people it happens to have never run a mile in their life. Others played sport for 30 years. It makes almost no difference because the load is not coming from what you did. It is coming from standing up and walking. Which is why most of what gets sold for this cannot work. A sleeve squeezes the whole knee and leaves that band carrying everything. A hinged brace stops the knee going sideways and it was never going sideways. Gel sits on the skin. A pain killer turns the alarm off and leaves the load exactly where it was. None of them are aimed at the spot. What that band actually needs is for less of your weight to land on it. That is what this does. It is called stride. It sits two centimeters below the kneecap on the tendon and never crosses the joint. A silicon pad inside holds pressure on that one band instead of spreading it around the whole knee. Your weight gets caught and moved off the worn part before it reaches the joint. The placement is the whole thing. A centimeter too high and it is a sleeve again. 34 % less strain measured. Three years with orthopedic surgeons. 200 ,000 people wearing one. 10 seconds to put on. No saws. No rolling down and nobody can see it. The thing people write to us about most is not the pain. It is that the knee stops feeling like a rusty hinge. They stop planning the stairs before they get to them. And you do not have to take my word for any of it. Put one on one knee only. Leave the other bear. Go to your own stairs and come down forwards. You will know in a minute. Not because the arthritis has gone. It is still there and nothing here changes that because the weight is not landing on that band anymore. So here is the choice. Keep aiming at the joint, which is where it hurts but not where the load is. Or move the load off the one spot that has been taking it since you were a teenager. Two for one. So you can do both knees. 60 days and you keep the straps from the stride site. The copy stretch and a stretched strap stops holding the spot. The next step is going to land in the same place either way. Go and do it forwards.",
